{"chunk_id": "2ab90f1e3e3f", "text": "Walton writes to his sister describing a bizarre event in the Arctic. While their ship is trapped among endless plains of ice, the crew spots a distant sledge racing northward, driven by a gigantic human-like figure. Soon after the ice breaks, they discover another sledge drifting on a floe carrying a nearly frozen European man\u2014Victor Frankenstein. Once revived, the stranger reveals he is pursuing the \u201cd\u00e6mon\u201d they glimpsed earlier. Walton becomes deeply fascinated by him: the man seems brilliant, sorrowful, and driven by terrible grief. When Walton passionately defends his own quest for glory and discovery, the stranger reacts with anguish and warns him against such ambition. Finally, he agrees to tell the tragic story that led him into the frozen wilderness, setting up the novel\u2019s central narrative and moral warning about dangerous pursuit of knowledge.\nWalton rescues the exhausted Victor Frankenstein pursuing a mysterious giant across the Arctic ice; Victor agrees to tell the tragic story of his downfall.\nThemes: obsessive pursuit, dangerous ambition, frame narrative, isolation, warning through experience", "vector": [0.009209309704601765, -0.02211831323802471, 0.006397942081093788, -0.01207058411091566, -0.022384479641914368, 0.02688266709446907, -0.03662430867552757, 0.008790099993348122, -0.003247213317081332, -0.011431788094341755, -0.010340511798858643, 0.03255198150873184, -0.006966869812458754, -0.0006828796467743814, 0.023169666528701782, 0.006880365777760744, 0.04301226884126663, -0.005646025761961937, 0.02351568080484867, -0.024527108296751976, -0.013734115287661552, 0.0067073586396873, 0.010047730058431625, 0.0017234185943379998, -0.023701995611190796, -0.009901339188218117, 0.03012988157570362, -0.04546098783612251, 0.0230898167937994, -0.03164702281355858, 0.007445966824889183, -7.9225683293771e-05, 0.00043667698628269136, -0.009169384837150574, -0.02286357618868351, 0.000498643552418798